### Part (a) — C Program to Reverse a String Without Any Library String Function

- **Two-Pointer Algorithm:** First scan manually until `str[len] == '\0'` to find length `len` without `strlen()`, then swap `str[left]` and `str[right]` while `left < right` (`O(n)` time, `O(1)` auxiliary space).

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    char str[100], temp;
    int len = 0, left, right;

    printf("Enter a word: ");
    if (scanf("%99s", str) != 1) return 1;

    while (str[len] != '\0') {
        len++;                          /* manual length count */
    }

    for (left = 0, right = len - 1; left < right; left++, right--) {
        temp = str[left];
        str[left] = str[right];
        str[right] = temp;
    }

    printf("Reversed string: %s\n", str);
    return 0;
}
```

- **Dry-Run (`"KOLKATA"`):** `len = 7`; swaps index pairs `(0,6)`, `(1,5)`, and `(2,4)` while leaving middle character `'K'` at index `3` untouched → prints `Reversed string: ATAKLOK` (the terminating `'\0'` at index `7` remains intact).

### Part (b) — Different Storage Classes in C with Examples

| Storage Class | Memory Area | Default Value | Scope & Linkage | Lifetime |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `auto` | Stack | Garbage | Local to block (no linkage) | Enclosing block execution |
| `register` | CPU register | Garbage | Local to block (`&` forbidden) | Enclosing block execution |
| `static` | Data / BSS | `0` | Local to block, or internal file linkage | Whole program execution |
| `extern` | Data / BSS | `0` | Global external linkage across `.c` files | Whole program execution |

- **Code Illustration:** `static int visits = 0; visits++;` inside a function initializes `visits` once and remembers its value across repeated calls, unlike `auto int visits = 0;` which resets to `0` every call.

> [!MARKS] 4 + 4 = 8 Marks (`a`: Address-of `&` and value-at `*` operators with example — 4m · `b`: C program accessing 1D array via pointers — 4m)

### Part (a) — "Address of" (`&`) and "Value at" (`*`) Pointer Operators

- **Address-of Operator (`&` — Referencing):** Unary operator applied to an lvalue variable (`&x`) that returns the **starting memory byte address** where `x` is stored (cannot be applied to constants, expressions like `&(x + 1)`, or `register` variables).
- **Value-at / Indirection Operator (`*` — Dereferencing):** Unary operator applied to a valid initialized pointer (`*p`) that accesses or modifies the **data value stored at address `p`** (`*&x` is identical to `x`; dereferencing an uninitialized or `NULL` pointer causes undefined behaviour).

```c
int x = 25;
int *p = &x;        /* &x gives address of x; stored in pointer p */
*p = 50;            /* *p dereferences p, changing x from 25 to 50 */
```

### Part (b) — C Program to Access a One-Dimensional Array Using Pointers

- **Pointer Arithmetic Rule:** Assigning `ptr = arr;` sets `ptr = &arr[0]`. For `int *ptr`, `ptr + i` advances by `i * sizeof(int)` bytes so `*(ptr + i)` reads or writes `arr[i]`.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int arr[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
    int *ptr = arr;                     /* points to arr[0] */
    int i;

    printf("Index  Address         Value\n");
    for (i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
        printf("arr[%d] %p  %d\n", i, (void *)(ptr + i), *(ptr + i));
    }
    return 0;
}
```

- **Sample Output:** Prints all five elements `10, 20, 30, 40, 50` alongside consecutive `sizeof(int)`-spaced addresses (for 4-byte `int` starting at `0x1000`, addresses step `0x1000, 0x1004, 0x1008, 0x100C, 0x1010`).

> [!MARKS] 4 + 4 = 8 Marks (`a`: Complete C program for Fibonacci series using recursion — 4m · `b`: Complete C program for Fibonacci series using loop — 4m)

### Part (a) — C Program to Generate Fibonacci Series Using Recursion

- **Recurrence:** `F(0) = 0`, `F(1) = 1` (base cases), and `F(k) = F(k - 1) + F(k - 2)` for `k >= 2` (naive tree recursion takes `O(2^n)` time and `O(n)` call-stack depth).

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int fib(int k) {
    if (k == 0) return 0;
    if (k == 1) return 1;
    return fib(k - 1) + fib(k - 2);
}

int main(void) {
    int n, i;
    printf("Enter number of terms: ");
    if (scanf("%d", &n) != 1 || n < 1) return 1;

    printf("Fibonacci series (recursive): ");
    for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        printf("%d ", fib(i));
    }
    printf("\n");
    return 0;
}
```

### Part (b) — C Program to Generate Fibonacci Series Using a Loop

- **Iterative State Update:** Track only the last two terms `(a, b)` initialized to `(0, 1)` and advance `next = a + b; a = b; b = next;` in `O(n)` time and `O(1)` auxiliary space.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int n, i;
    long a = 0, b = 1, next;

    printf("Enter number of terms: ");
    if (scanf("%d", &n) != 1 || n < 1) return 1;

    printf("Fibonacci series (iterative): ");
    for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        printf("%ld ", a);
        next = a + b;
        a = b;
        b = next;
    }
    printf("\n");
    return 0;
}
```

- **Sample Output (`n = 7` for both programs):** `0 1 1 2 3 5 8` — while both produce identical sequences, the iterative loop avoids redundant overlapping sub-calls (`fib(2)` recomputed repeatedly) and runs exponentially faster for larger `n`.

> [!MARKS] 4 + 4 = 8 Marks (`a`: Complete C program illustrating `switch-case` — 4m · `b`: Complete C program to find Armstrong numbers in `100..1000` — 4m)

### Part (a) — Illustrating `switch-case` with an Example C Program

- **Mechanism:** `switch` evaluates an integer/character selector once and dispatches control to the matching constant `case` label (`break;` prevents fall-through; `default:` handles invalid choices).

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    char op;
    int a = 12, b = 4;
    printf("Enter operator (+, -, *, /): ");
    if (scanf(" %c", &op) != 1) return 1;

    switch (op) {
        case '+': printf("Result = %d\n", a + b); break;
        case '-': printf("Result = %d\n", a - b); break;
        case '*': printf("Result = %d\n", a * b); break;
        case '/': printf("Result = %d\n", a / b); break;
        default:  printf("Invalid operator\n");   break;
    }
    return 0;
}
```

### Part (b) — C Program to Print All Armstrong Numbers from `100` to `1000`

- **Armstrong (Narcissistic) Number Rule:** A `k`-digit positive integer equals the sum of the `k`-th powers of its digits (for `3`-digit numbers `100..999`, `d₁³ + d₂³ + d₃³ == n`; for `4`-digit `1000`, `1⁴ + 0⁴ + 0⁴ + 0⁴ = 1 != 1000`).

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int int_pow(int base, int exp) {
    int p = 1;
    while (exp-- > 0) p *= base;
    return p;
}

int main(void) {
    int n;
    printf("Armstrong numbers between 100 and 1000: ");
    for (n = 100; n <= 1000; n++) {
        int temp = n, digits = 0, sum = 0;
        while (temp > 0) { digits++; temp /= 10; }
        temp = n;
        while (temp > 0) {
            sum += int_pow(temp % 10, digits);
            temp /= 10;
        }
        if (sum == n) {
            printf("%d ", n);
        }
    }
    printf("\n");
    return 0;
}
```

- **Verified Output:** `Armstrong numbers between 100 and 1000: 153 370 371 407` (e.g., `1³ + 5³ + 3³ = 1 + 125 + 27 = 153`).
